# Schema convention, version [[ convention_version ]]

The rules this project's warehouse follows, so that dbt, the Apache Ossie [[ ossie_spec ]]
semantic model under `osi/` and the MCP server agree on names. The tests and macros that enforce
them ship in the `vecten_convention` dbt package, installed with vecten, not copied here.
[% if 'platform' not in components %]
This project runs only the MCP over an existing warehouse; the dbt parts apply to its builders.
[% endif %]
## Schemas

`raw_<source>`, `staging`, `snapshots`, `core`, `marts`, and `core_<area>` and `marts_<area>`
for each restricted area `vecten.toml` declares as `[areas.<area>]`; `<area>` is lowercase
letters and digits, starting with a letter. The names are the same on Postgres and Snowflake and
never take the profile's target schema as a prefix. dlt writes `raw_<source>`; dbt writes the
others and never writes into a raw schema.

## Model names

| Layer | Shape | Example |
|---|---|---|
| staging | `staging_<source>_<entity>` | `staging_hubspot_company` |
| snapshots | `snapshot_<source>_<entity>` | `snapshot_hubspot_company` |
| core | `<entity>`, a singular noun | `company`, `contact`, `deal` |
| marts | `<domain>_<subject>` | `sales_pipeline` |

Names are lowercase letters and digits joined by single underscores, never start with a digit,
and use full words, never abbreviations. The test `convention_model_naming` checks what it can:
the `staging_` and `snapshot_` prefixes with a source and an entity segment, the character rules,
and that every model lands in one of the four dbt schemas or a declared area's. It cannot tell an
abbreviation from a word or a plural from a singular, so `companies` passes the test and is still
wrong.

## Layers

- **Staging.** One view per raw table. Cast every raw column to an explicit type, since dlt
  keeps ISO timestamps and dates as text, rename to the convention, and add `_loaded_at`
  (UTC) and `_source` (the source name).
- **Snapshots.** One per staging model, `strategy='check'` with `check_cols` listing the
  business columns only; `_loaded_at` changes on every build and would version every row.
- **Core.** One table per entity, read from the snapshots' current rows
  (`where dbt_valid_to is null`).
- **Marts.** Tables for one domain, built from core, named with the domain first. Every mart is
  one dataset in the semantic model.

## Restricted areas

An area is a pair of schemas that no role reads until an admin grants the area to a group, which
then reads both. Its models live in `dbt/models/core_<area>/` and `dbt/models/marts_<area>/`,
each folder configured in `dbt/dbt_project.yml` with `+schema` set to its schema. A model in
`core_<area>` follows every core rule and one in `marts_<area>` every marts rule, so each mart
there is a dataset too. A core model whose rows the default group must not read goes to
`core_<area>`; one that unrestricted marts also read stays in `core`. A model in a `core_<name>`
or `marts_<name>` schema whose area `vecten.toml` does not declare fails `convention_model_naming`.

## Descriptions and keys

Every core and mart model has a description, and every column it builds is declared in its
`schema.yml` with a description; a built column missing from `schema.yml` fails the build. A
description has one home, `schema.yml`; `vecten dbt-build-local` copies it into `osi/`.
Every core and mart model declares a primary key: a column carrying both the `unique` and the
`not_null` data test, or, for a mart's composite key, `meta.osi.primary_key: [country, stage]` in
the model's `config`. Marts need one because dbt requires an entity on every semantic model.

The convention tests read the whole graph and the built relations rather than `ref()`, so they
run as a second step after the build:

    dbt build --exclude package:vecten_convention,test_type:singular
    dbt test  --select  package:vecten_convention,test_type:singular

`vecten dbt-build-local` and the `dbt_build` DAG run both; `vecten dbt build` runs what you pass.
Each of them passes the areas `vecten.toml` declares as the dbt var `vecten_areas`, which plain
`dbt` needs as `--vars '{"vecten_areas": ["finance"]}'`, or the naming test reports every area
model undeclared. A violation of
`convention_model_naming`, `convention_descriptions`, `convention_core_primary_key`,
`convention_staging_load_metadata` or `convention_no_grants` reads `model <name>: <reason>`
(`snapshot` or `seed` in place of `model` for those).

The MCP owns every read grant, so the project declares none: `convention_no_grants` fails a model,
snapshot or seed, the project's or a dbt package's, that sets dbt's `grants` config. On Snowflake
`dbt/dbt_project.yml` sets `+copy_grants: true`, so a rebuilt relation keeps the grants of the
one it replaces, and the test fails a model that sets `copy_grants` to `false`. The same check
runs at the start of every dbt run, which stops before anything is built.

## Identity macros

- `vecten_convention.normalize_domain(column)`: lowercase, strip the protocol, a leading `www.`
  and anything from the first `/` or `?`; empty becomes null.
- `vecten_convention.normalize_email(column)`: lowercase and trim; empty becomes null.
- `vecten_convention.add_days(date_expression, days)`: date arithmetic in either dialect.

Each returns the same value for the same input on Postgres and Snowflake.

## Time

Every timestamp is UTC; timestamp columns end in `_at`, date columns in `_date`. dbt requires a
time spine once a metric exists; the convention package ships `metricflow_time_spine` in `core`
(one row per day from 2000-01-01), so the project defines none.

## Ossie documents

Every file under `osi/` except hidden files is a JSON Ossie document, version `[[ ossie_spec ]]`:

    {"version": "[[ ossie_spec ]]", "semantic_model": [{"name": "...", "datasets": [], "metrics": [], "relationships": []}]}

dbt parses the same files through `osi-paths` in `dbt/dbt_project.yml` (`../osi`, or
`VECTEN_OSI_PATH` when set). `vecten dbt-build-local` builds against an empty directory and then
checks the real `osi/` with `dbt parse`. To rename or drop a mart a metric uses, edit or remove
the metric in `osi/metrics.json`, then run `vecten dbt-build-local`. dbt 1.12 prints
`CustomTopLevelKeyDeprecation` for `osi-paths` on every run: dbt's project schema lags its own
feature, and the warning is harmless.

[% if 'platform' in components %]- `osi/datasets.json` is generated by `vecten dbt-build-local` after a green build and never
  edited by hand. CI runs `vecten dbt-build-local --check` and fails when it is stale.
[% else %]- `osi/datasets.json` is drafted by `vecten mcp introspect` and refined by hand; the rules
  below say what a generated dataset looks like.
[% endif %]- `osi/metrics.json` holds the metrics and is written by hand. Every column in a metric
  expression is qualified with its dataset name, `SUM(sales_pipeline.amount)`, and a metric
  reads one dataset.
- The pinned schema closes the vendor list, so the Vecten extension uses `vendor_name: COMMON`
  with `data` a JSON string holding a `vecten` object:
  `{"vendor_name": "COMMON", "data": "{\"vecten\": {\"dataset\": \"sales_pipeline\"}}"}`.
  On a metric it can name the home `dataset` or declare `additivity`.
- Expressions use the `ANSI_SQL` dialect; Postgres projects always use it and the schema has no
  Postgres dialect. On Snowflake a `SNOWFLAKE` entry is used when present, `ANSI_SQL` otherwise.

The generator writes one dataset per mart, ordered by name:

- `source` is `<database>.<schema>.<alias>`, the schema being `marts` or the mart's
  `marts_<area>`, and the database `[warehouse] database` in `vecten.toml`, or `warehouse` when
  unset; the local build uses the same database name. Two marts of one alias are refused.
- Fields are the columns declared in `schema.yml`, in order, with their descriptions;
  `primary_key` is `meta.osi.primary_key` or else the columns tested `unique` and `not_null`.
- A column named `*_at` or `*_date`, or with a date or timestamp type, is a time dimension. A
  column in the primary key or named `*_id` is a dimension whatever its type. Other numeric
  columns are facts; everything else is a dimension.
- `meta.osi.synonyms` on a column or a model becomes its `ai_context.synonyms`.
- A mart descending from a `snapshot_` model carries `snapshot_derived` in the Vecten extension;
  `vecten mcp validate` warns on a summed metric over it that declares no additivity.

## What a change to this convention means

The number in this file's title is the convention version, also `convention_version` in
`vecten.toml`. A change to any rule is a new version, bumped here in the same change as the
convention tests and generator change it needs; a project takes it with the vecten release that
ships them, never by editing this file.
